{
  "schemaVersion": 4,
  "id": "t02-012",
  "track": "track-02",
  "title": "ASP.NET Core Identity accounts cookies bearer tokens OpenID Connect and passkeys",
  "status": "source-backed-draft-generation-enabled",
  "inventoryComplete": true,
  "concepts": [
    "ASP.NET Core Identity provides account and credential management around user and optional role stores. It is not itself an OAuth authorization server; its built-in API bearer tokens are proprietary Identity tokens, not standards-compliant JWT access tokens.",
    "Cookie authentication protects a browser session with a Data Protection-protected ticket. Expiry, persistence, renewal, cookie attributes, shared key rings, and event handlers all affect the security and user experience of that session.",
    "A JWT bearer handler validates an access token issued for the API: signature, issuer, audience, and lifetime must be checked. An ID token is for the client to learn that a user authenticated and must not be presented as an API access token.",
    "For interactive server web apps, use the OpenID Connect authorization-code flow with PKCE and a server-side confidential client. External identity providers authenticate users, while the application still performs local authorization and maps only the claims it trusts.",
    "ASP.NET Core Identity supports WebAuthn passkeys in current releases. Passkeys use a relying-party domain and public-key credentials; the built-in implementation covers Identity authentication scenarios rather than acting as a general-purpose WebAuthn framework."
  ],
